Using Git Integration with Jenkins
Lab Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Install and configure Jenkins on a Linux machine
Set up a GitHub repository for continuous integration
Configure Jenkins to automatically trigger builds when code is pushed to GitHub
Create a Jenkins pipeline that integrates with a Git repository
Understand webhook configuration for automated build triggering
Troubleshoot common Git-Jenkins integration issues
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Git version control concepts
Familiarity with Linux command line operations
Basic knowledge of software development workflows
Understanding of what continuous integration means
A GitHub account (free account is sufficient)
Lab Environment
Al Nafi provides Linux-based cloud machines for this lab. Simply click Start Lab to access your dedicated Linux machine. The provided machine is bare metal with no pre-installed tools, so you will install all required software during the lab exercises.

Task 1: Environment Setup and Jenkins Installation
Subtask 1.1: Update System and Install Prerequisites
First, update your Linux system and install the necessary dependencies.

# Update package lists
sudo apt update

# Install Java (required for Jenkins)
sudo apt install -y openjdk-11-jdk

# Verify Java installation
java -version

# Install curl and wget for downloading packages
sudo apt install -y curl wget gnupg2 software-properties-common
Subtask 1.2: Install Jenkins
Install Jenkins using the official repository to ensure you get the latest stable version.

# Add Jenkins repository key
curl -fsSL https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable/jenkins.io-2023.key | sudo tee \
  /usr/share/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc > /dev/null

# Add Jenkins repository
echo deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc] \
  https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable binary/ | sudo tee \
  /etc/apt/sources.list.d/jenkins.list > /dev/null

# Update package lists
sudo apt update

# Install Jenkins
sudo apt install -y jenkins

# Start Jenkins service
sudo systemctl start jenkins

# Enable Jenkins to start on boot
sudo systemctl enable jenkins

# Check Jenkins status
sudo systemctl status jenkins
Subtask 1.3: Configure Jenkins Initial Setup
Access Jenkins through the web interface and complete the initial setup.

# Get the initial admin password
sudo cat /var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword
Open a web browser and navigate to http://localhost:8080
Enter the initial admin password you retrieved above
Click Install suggested plugins
Wait for the plugin installation to complete
Create your first admin user with the following details:
Username: admin
Password: admin123
Full name: Jenkins Admin
Email: admin@localhost
Keep the default Jenkins URL as http://localhost:8080/
Click Save and Finish
Subtask 1.4: Install Git on the System
Install Git which will be used by Jenkins to interact with repositories.

# Install Git
sudo apt install -y git

# Verify Git installation
git --version

# Configure Git with basic settings
git config --global user.name "Jenkins User"
git config --global user.email "jenkins@localhost"
Task 2: Create and Configure GitHub Repository
Subtask 2.1: Create a Sample Project Repository
Log in to your GitHub account
Click the + icon in the top right corner
Select New repository
Configure the repository:
Repository name: jenkins-integration-demo
Description: Demo project for Jenkins integration
Set to Public
Check Add a README file
Add .gitignore template: Node
Choose license: MIT License
Click Create repository
Subtask 2.2: Clone Repository and Add Sample Code
Clone the repository to your local machine and add a simple application.

# Navigate to home directory
cd ~

# Clone your repository (replace YOUR_USERNAME with your GitHub username)
git clone https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/jenkins-integration-demo.git

# Navigate to the project directory
cd jenkins-integration-demo

# Create a simple Node.js application
cat > app.js << 'EOF'
const http = require('http');

const hostname = '127.0.0.1';
const port = 3000;

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.statusCode = 200;
  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/plain');
  res.end('Hello World from Jenkins Integration Demo!\n');
});

server.listen(port, hostname, () => {
  console.log(`Server running at http://${hostname}:${port}/`);
});
EOF

# Create package.json file
cat > package.json << 'EOF'
{
  "name": "jenkins-integration-demo",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "description": "Demo project for Jenkins integration",
  "main": "app.js",
  "scripts": {
    "start": "node app.js",
    "test": "echo \"Running tests...\" && echo \"All tests passed!\""
  },
  "author": "Jenkins User",
  "license": "MIT"
}
EOF

# Create a simple test script
mkdir -p test
cat > test/app.test.js << 'EOF'
// Simple test file
console.log('Running application tests...');

// Simulate test execution
setTimeout(() => {
  console.log('Test 1: Application starts successfully - PASSED');
  console.log('Test 2: Server responds to requests - PASSED');
  console.log('All tests completed successfully!');
  process.exit(0);
}, 1000);
EOF
Subtask 2.3: Commit and Push Initial Code
# Add all files to Git
git add .

# Commit the changes
git commit -m "Initial commit: Add Node.js application with tests"

# Push to GitHub
git push origin main
Task 3: Configure Jenkins Job for Git Integration
Subtask 3.1: Install Required Jenkins Plugins
In Jenkins web interface, go to Manage Jenkins > Manage Plugins
Click on the Available tab
Search for and install the following plugins:
Git plugin (if not already installed)
GitHub plugin
GitHub Integration Plugin
NodeJS Plugin
Check Restart Jenkins when installation is complete and no jobs are running
Wait for Jenkins to restart
Subtask 3.2: Configure NodeJS in Jenkins
Go to Manage Jenkins > Global Tool Configuration
Scroll down to NodeJS section
Click Add NodeJS
Configure:
Name: NodeJS-16
Check Install automatically
Version: Select latest LTS version (e.g., NodeJS 16.20.0)
Click Save
Subtask 3.3: Create a New Jenkins Job
From Jenkins dashboard, click New Item
Enter item name: jenkins-git-integration-demo
Select Freestyle project
Click OK
Subtask 3.4: Configure Source Code Management
In the job configuration page, scroll to Source Code Management
Select Git
In Repository URL, enter your GitHub repository URL:
https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/jenkins-integration-demo.git
Leave Credentials as - none - (for public repositories)
In Branches to build, ensure it shows */main
Subtask 3.5: Configure Build Environment
In Build Environment section, check Provide Node & npm bin/ folder to PATH
Select NodeJS-16 from the dropdown
Subtask 3.6: Add Build Steps
In Build section, click Add build step > Execute shell
Enter the following build script:
#!/bin/bash
echo "Starting build process..."
echo "Current directory: $(pwd)"
echo "Files in directory:"
ls -la

echo "Installing dependencies..."
npm install

echo "Running tests..."
npm test

echo "Starting application (background process for testing)..."
timeout 10s npm start &
APP_PID=$!

echo "Waiting for application to start..."
sleep 3

echo "Testing application endpoint..."
curl -f http://127.0.0.1:3000 || echo "Application test failed"

echo "Stopping application..."
kill $APP_PID 2>/dev/null || true

echo "Build completed successfully!"
Click Save
Subtask 3.7: Test Manual Build
From the job page, click Build Now
Click on the build number in Build History
Click Console Output to view the build logs
Verify that the build completes successfully
Task 4: Set Up Automated Git Push Triggers
Subtask 4.1: Configure Webhook in GitHub
Go to your GitHub repository
Click on Settings tab
Click on Webhooks in the left sidebar
Click Add webhook
Configure the webhook:
Payload URL: http://YOUR_JENKINS_IP:8080/github-webhook/
Content type: application/json
Which events: Select Just the push event
Active: Check this box
Click Add webhook
Note: Since we're working on a local machine, GitHub cannot directly reach your Jenkins instance. For this lab, we'll simulate the webhook functionality using polling.

Subtask 4.2: Configure Jenkins for Git Polling
Go back to your Jenkins job configuration
In Build Triggers section, check Poll SCM
In the Schedule field, enter:
H/2 * * * *
This will check for changes every 2 minutes.
Click Save
